Python bindings: do not call PySys_Write* from worker threads

Upstream bug (Psychtoolbox-3 3.0.22.2, Python bindings only).
PsychConstants.h maps printf to PySys_WriteStdout and fprintf to
PySys_WriteStderr for PSYCH_LANGUAGE == PSYCH_PYTHON. Both require the
calling thread to hold the GIL. Worker threads created with
PsychCreateThread() do not hold it, but they print: for example
KbQueueWorkerThreadMain() (Linux PsychHID) calls PsychSetThreadPriority(),
whose failure message (Linux/Base/PsychTimeGlue.c) is a printf. On a
system without an RLIMIT_RTPRIO grant, pthread_setschedparam() fails, the
thread calls PySys_WriteStdout() without the GIL, and Python crashes with
SIGSEGV in sys_write(). PsychoPy's default keyboard backend
(psychopy.hardware.keyboard.Keyboard -> PsychHID KbQueue) triggers this,
so Builder experiments crash on start.

Fix: on non-master threads, print to the C stderr stream, like the Matlab
branch of the same header already gates mexPrintf on PsychIsMasterThread().

--- a/PsychSourceGL/Source/Common/Base/PsychConstants.h
+++ b/PsychSourceGL/Source/Common/Base/PsychConstants.h
@@ -69,10 +69,17 @@
 #endif
 
 #if PSYCH_LANGUAGE == PSYCH_PYTHON
+    // PySys_WriteStdout() and PySys_WriteStderr() require the calling thread
+    // to hold the GIL. Only the interpreter thread that runs a module function
+    // holds it. Worker threads (PsychHID KbQueue, IOPort reader, PortAudio
+    // callback) print too, for example when PsychSetThreadPriority() fails
+    // without realtime permissions, and calling into Python from there crashes
+    // the interpreter. Send their output to the C stderr stream instead.
+    // The parentheses in (fprintf) suppress expansion of the fprintf macro.
     #undef printf
-    #define printf PySys_WriteStdout
+    #define printf(...) (PsychIsMasterThread() ? PySys_WriteStdout(__VA_ARGS__) : (void) (fprintf)(stderr, __VA_ARGS__))
     #undef fprintf
-    #define fprintf(fdignore, ...) PySys_WriteStderr(__VA_ARGS__)
+    #define fprintf(fdignore, ...) (PsychIsMasterThread() ? PySys_WriteStderr(__VA_ARGS__) : (void) (fprintf)(stderr, __VA_ARGS__))
 #endif
 
 //platform dependent macro defines
